diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 967d66e..8752236 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -9,6 +9,16 @@ plugins. It exposes a backend abstraction for graph queries, with current backen
 The query interface is built around openCypher, including a PostgreSQL SQL translator for environments that do not
 support Cypher natively.
 
+## Ordinary Cypher calculations
+
+Ordinary variable-length matches without an upper bound now follow paths beyond fifteen links. Explicit bounds still apply, and the existing shortest-path default is unchanged. The aggregate-count shortcut is used only when the query supplies a bound; it must not silently truncate an unbounded query.
+
+The shared `ordinary_cypher_shapes.json` integration cases cover long paths, inputs carried through `WITH` and optional matches, Boolean fields in parameter and collected maps, parameter names reused as column names, values used by `SET`, and sorting by a returned column that replaces an earlier object name. They run with the same expected answers on both databases. PostgreSQL is still a supported subset of Cypher, not every Neo4j feature; multiple writes separated by `WITH` remain an unsupported form in some queries.
+
+A later link that ends at an already selected object now reuses that object directly. Previously, PostgreSQL could add an unrelated node lookup and multiply a path count by every node in the graph. Tests cover required and optional paths, both directions, property restrictions, saving the count, and a valid undirected self loop. This is an automatic correctness fix; no query instruction is required.
+
+Run `make test_all` once for each database using its `CONNECTION_STRING`. The template cases use the `manual_integration` build tag included by that command. These tests are separate from the BloodHound extension comparison and its generated-data measurements.
+
 ## Quick Start
 
 Build the repository:
